/* Modules 1-5. Original study text. [[Term]] or [[Glossary Term|text shown]] creates a glossary popup link. */
M({
  id: 1,
  title: "The Real Estate Industry and Career Path",
  summary: "What real estate professionals do, how the market works, the main types of property, and what a career as a licensed salesperson looks like.",
  objectives: [
    "Describe the main specialties within the real estate business.",
    "Explain the difference between a salesperson and a broker.",
    "Identify the forces of supply and demand that move real estate prices.",
    "Distinguish an independent contractor from an employee for tax purposes.",
    "Recognize the major professional organizations and what membership means."
  ],
  sections: [
    ["A business with many specialties", `<p>Real estate is much bigger than selling houses. Many different professionals touch a single property over its lifetime, and each needs a different skill set.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Brokerage</strong> brings buyers and sellers (or landlords and tenants) together for a fee. This is where most new licensees start.</li>
<li><strong>Appraisal</strong> estimates a property's value. Appraisers are licensed or certified separately from salespeople.</li>
<li><strong>Property management</strong> runs rental property for an owner: finding tenants, collecting rent and arranging maintenance.</li>
<li><strong>Financing</strong> professionals such as mortgage loan originators help buyers borrow money.</li>
<li><strong>Development and construction</strong> turn raw land into usable property.</li>
<li><strong>Counseling, education and investment</strong> round out the field.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every state requires a license to perform [[brokerage]] activities for others in exchange for compensation.</p>`],
    ["Salesperson versus broker", `<p>States usually have two license levels.</p>
<ul>
<li>A [[salesperson]] (also called a sales associate or agent) is licensed to perform brokerage activities only <em>on behalf of a sponsoring broker</em>. A salesperson cannot run an independent business, cannot hold client money in their own account, and is paid only through their broker.</li>
<li>A [[broker]] holds a higher license that requires more education and experience. A broker can open a brokerage firm, supervise salespeople and hold [[escrow]] (trust) funds.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every listing, buyer agreement and commission legally belongs to the broker, not to the individual salesperson. If you change firms, your listings normally stay with your old broker.</p>`],
    ["How the real estate market works", `<p>Prices are set by [[supply and demand]]. Because land cannot be moved, real estate markets are <strong>local</strong>: a shortage of homes in one city does nothing for buyers in another state.</p>
<ul>
<li>When demand rises faster than supply, prices rise and homes sell quickly. That is a [[seller's market]].</li>
<li>When supply outpaces demand, prices soften, homes sit longer and buyers can negotiate. That is a [[buyer's market]].</li>
</ul>
<p>Supply responds slowly because new construction takes months or years. Factors that shift supply include labor, materials, interest rates and government controls such as zoning. Factors that shift demand include population, employment, wages and mortgage rates.</p>`],
    ["Types of property and housing", `<p>Licensing exams often ask you to classify property by its use:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Residential</strong>: places where people live, including single-family homes, apartments, condominiums and manufactured housing.</li>
<li><strong>Commercial</strong>: office buildings, retail stores and other business property.</li>
<li><strong>Industrial</strong>: factories, warehouses and research facilities.</li>
<li><strong>Agricultural</strong>: farms, ranches, orchards and timberland.</li>
<li><strong>Special purpose</strong>: churches, schools, cemeteries and government buildings.</li>
</ul>
<p>Common housing types include detached single-family homes, townhouses, condominiums, cooperatives, mixed-use developments, manufactured homes and multifamily buildings.</p>`],
    ["Working as a licensee", `<p>Most salespeople are [[independent contractor|independent contractors]], not [[employee|employees]]. Under federal tax rules a licensee is treated as an independent contractor when three conditions are met:</p>
<ol>
<li>The person holds a real estate license.</li>
<li>Substantially all pay is based on sales production (commissions), not hours worked.</li>
<li>A written agreement states the person will not be treated as an employee for tax purposes.</li>
</ol>
<p>Independent contractors pay their own self-employment taxes and set their own hours and methods. A broker can still require compliance with the law and office policy, but cannot dictate daily schedules the way an employer can.</p>
<p>Your income comes from a [[commission]], which is negotiable between the client and the brokerage. No law or association sets a standard rate, and agreeing with competitors to fix rates is illegal (see Module 8).</p>`],
    ["Professional organizations", `<p>The National Association of REALTORS® (NAR) is the largest trade group. Only members may call themselves a [[REALTOR®]]; the word is a registered trademark, not a job title. Members agree to follow the NAR Code of Ethics, which is stricter than the law in some areas.</p>
<p>Other groups include the National Association of Real Estate Brokers (members are called Realtists), and many specialty institutes for appraisers, property managers and commercial practitioners. Membership is voluntary; your license comes from the state, not from any association.</p>`]
  ],
  terms: [
    ["Brokerage", "The business of bringing parties together in a real estate transaction for a fee."],
    ["Broker", "A person licensed to run a real estate business, supervise salespeople and hold client funds."],
    ["Salesperson", "A licensee who may perform brokerage activities only under the supervision of a sponsoring broker."],
    ["Supply and demand", "The economic principle that prices rise when demand exceeds supply and fall when supply exceeds demand."],
    ["Seller's market", "A market where buyers outnumber available homes, so prices rise and sellers have the advantage."],
    ["Buyer's market", "A market where available homes outnumber buyers, so prices soften and buyers have the advantage."],
    ["Independent contractor", "A worker paid by results who controls their own methods and pays their own taxes; most salespeople are treated this way."],
    ["Employee", "A worker whose employer controls how and when work is done and withholds taxes from pay."],
    ["Commission", "A fee for brokerage services, usually a percentage of the sale price, that is fully negotiable."],
    ["REALTOR®", "A registered trademark that only members of the National Association of REALTORS® may use."],
    ["Escrow", "Money or documents held by a neutral party until the conditions of an agreement are met; brokers hold escrow funds in trust accounts."]
  ],
  scenarios: [
    ["Who owns the listing?", "Dana, a salesperson at Peach Realty, signs a listing with a seller. Two months later Dana moves to another brokerage and assumes the listing goes along. It does not. The listing agreement is a contract between the seller and Peach Realty, so the listing stays with Peach Realty unless the broker and the seller agree to release it. Dana can only be paid for that listing through the broker who held her license when it was earned."]
  ],
  traps: [
    "A salesperson never receives a commission directly from a client or another brokerage. All pay flows through the sponsoring [[broker]].",
    "There is no “standard” commission rate. If an answer says the rate is set by law, by the MLS or by an association, it is wrong.",
    "“REALTOR®” is not a synonym for “real estate agent.” It refers only to NAR members.",
    "Real estate markets are local because land is immobile. National averages do not set local prices.",
    "Independent contractor status depends on a license, pay based on production and a written agreement. Missing any one of these can make the person an employee."
  ],
  quiz: [
    ["A licensee who may perform brokerage activities only under the supervision of a sponsoring broker is a:", ["Salesperson", "Principal broker", "Appraiser", "Mortgage broker"], 0, "A [[salesperson]] must be sponsored and supervised by a [[broker]] and is paid only through that broker."],
    ["Who owns a listing agreement taken by a salesperson?", ["The salesperson who took it", "The sponsoring broker", "The multiple listing service", "The state real estate commission"], 1, "Listing agreements are contracts between the client and the brokerage, so they belong to the [[broker]]."],
    ["When the number of homes for sale greatly exceeds the number of buyers, the result is usually:", ["A seller's market", "Rising prices", "A buyer's market", "Rent control"], 2, "Excess supply relative to demand gives buyers bargaining power, creating a [[buyer's market]]."],
    ["Why are real estate markets described as local?", ["Because each county sets its own prices", "Because land is immobile", "Because lenders only operate locally", "Because the federal government regulates prices"], 1, "Immobility means a shortage in one area cannot be fixed by moving land in from another area."],
    ["Which is NOT one of the federal tests for treating a salesperson as an independent contractor?", ["The person holds a real estate license", "Pay is based on production rather than hours", "There is a written agreement stating independent contractor status", "The broker sets the person's daily work schedule"], 3, "Controlling the daily schedule points toward employee status. The three tests are license, production-based pay and a written agreement."],
    ["The commission rate on a home sale is set by:", ["State law", "The local REALTOR® association", "Negotiation between the client and the brokerage", "The multiple listing service"], 2, "Commission is always negotiable. Any rate set by law or agreement among competitors would be illegal price fixing."],
    ["The term REALTOR® may be used by:", ["Any licensed salesperson", "Any licensed broker", "Only members of the National Association of REALTORS®", "Only brokers with 5+ years of experience"], 2, "REALTOR® is a registered trademark limited to NAR members."],
    ["A warehouse used for distribution is classified as:", ["Commercial", "Industrial", "Special purpose", "Agricultural"], 1, "Warehouses, factories and distribution centers are industrial property."],
    ["A church or public school is an example of which property type?", ["Special purpose", "Commercial", "Residential", "Industrial"], 0, "Property built for a narrow use such as worship, education or government is special purpose."],
    ["Which statement about supply in real estate is true?", ["Supply adjusts instantly to demand", "Supply responds slowly because construction takes time", "Supply is fixed by the federal government", "Supply never affects price"], 1, "New buildings take months or years to complete, so supply lags behind changes in demand."]
  ]
});

M({
  id: 2,
  title: "Real Property, Personal Property, and the Law",
  summary: "The difference between land, real estate and real property; the bundle of rights; fixtures versus personal property; and where real estate law comes from.",
  objectives: [
    "Define land, real estate and real property and explain how they differ.",
    "List the rights in the bundle of legal rights.",
    "Apply the legal tests that decide whether an item is a fixture.",
    "Describe air, surface, subsurface and water rights.",
    "Identify the physical and economic characteristics of land."
  ],
  sections: [
    ["Land, real estate and real property", `<p>These three terms build on each other:</p>
<ul>
<li>[[Land]] is the earth's surface, everything below it down to the center of the earth, and the air space above it, including natural things like trees and water.</li>
<li>[[Real estate]] is land <em>plus</em> all permanent man-made [[improvement|improvements]] attached to it, such as houses, fences and sidewalks.</li>
<li>[[Real property]] is real estate <em>plus</em> the legal rights of ownership. In everyday speech and on most exams, “real estate” and “real property” are used interchangeably.</li>
</ul>
<p>Anything that is not real property is [[personal property]] (also called personalty or chattels). Personal property is movable: furniture, cars, a refrigerator that just plugs in.</p>`],
    ["The bundle of legal rights", `<p>Ownership is often pictured as a [[bundle of legal rights]]. An owner can:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Possess</strong> the property.</li>
<li><strong>Control</strong> its use within the law.</li>
<li><strong>Enjoy</strong> it without interference.</li>
<li><strong>Exclude</strong> others from it.</li>
<li><strong>Dispose</strong> of it by selling, giving, leasing or willing it.</li>
</ul>
<p>An owner can separate sticks from the bundle. Leasing a home transfers possession to a tenant while the owner keeps the right to dispose of it.</p>
<p>Ownership rights also extend up and down. [[Air rights]] cover the space above the surface (subject to aircraft flight). [[Subsurface rights]], also called mineral rights, cover what lies below. An owner can sell the minerals to one party and the surface to another.</p>`],
    ["Water rights", `<p>Who owns water depends on the state's legal doctrine:</p>
<ul>
<li>[[Riparian rights]] belong to owners of land bordering a flowing river or stream. They may make reasonable use of the water but cannot block or pollute it for neighbors downstream.</li>
<li>[[Littoral rights]] belong to owners of land bordering a large, still body of water such as a lake or ocean. They generally own to the average high-water mark.</li>
<li>[[Prior appropriation]] is used in dry western states. The state controls water and grants rights to the first person who puts it to beneficial use.</li>
</ul>
<p>Land can grow by <strong>accretion</strong> (slow buildup of soil, called alluvion) or shrink by <strong>erosion</strong>. Sudden loss of land from a storm or flood is <strong>avulsion</strong>, and the owner may be able to reclaim it.</p>`],
    ["Fixtures: when personal property becomes real property", `<p>A [[fixture]] is an item that was personal property but became real property by being attached to land or a building. A ceiling fan in a box is personal property; once wired into the ceiling it is usually a fixture and transfers with the house.</p>
<p>Courts use five tests, often remembered as <strong>MARIA</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>M</strong>ethod of annexation: how permanently is it attached? Would removing it cause damage?</li>
<li><strong>A</strong>daptation to the real estate: was it custom-made for this property, like fitted storm windows?</li>
<li><strong>R</strong>elationship of the parties: courts tend to favor buyers over sellers and tenants over landlords.</li>
<li><strong>I</strong>ntention: did the person who attached it intend it to stay? This is often the most important test.</li>
<li><strong>A</strong>greement: a written agreement between the parties controls over all the other tests.</li>
</ul>
<p>Attaching personal property to real property is <strong>annexation</strong>. Turning real property into personal property by removing it (cutting timber, harvesting a crop) is [[severance]].</p>
<p>Two special rules: [[trade fixtures]] installed by a commercial tenant for business use (shelving, ovens, salon chairs) stay the tenant's personal property and may be removed before the lease ends. Annual crops planted by a tenant farmer, called [[emblements]], belong to the tenant who planted them, even if the lease ends before harvest.</p>`],
    ["Characteristics of land", `<p><strong>Physical characteristics</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Immobility</strong>: land cannot be moved. This is why real estate is regulated and taxed locally.</li>
<li><strong>Indestructibility</strong>: land itself is permanent, although buildings wear out.</li>
<li><strong>Uniqueness</strong> (nonhomogeneity): no two parcels are identical. Because of this, a buyer can ask a court to force a seller to complete a sale through specific performance.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Economic characteristics</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Scarcity</strong>: the supply of usable land in a given area is limited.</li>
<li><strong>Improvements</strong>: building on one parcel changes the value of nearby parcels.</li>
<li><strong>Permanence of investment</strong>: money spent on roads, sewers and buildings is long-term.</li>
<li><strong>Area preference</strong> (situs): people's preference for a location. It is the most important economic factor in value.</li>
</ul>`],
    ["Where real estate law comes from", `<p>Real estate is governed by several layers of law: the U.S. and state constitutions, federal and state <strong>statutes</strong> passed by legislatures, <strong>common law</strong> (rules developed by court decisions over centuries), and <strong>administrative regulations</strong> written by agencies such as a state real estate commission. Each state's [[license law]] sets who must be licensed and how licensees must behave.</p>
<p>A real estate licensee must never practice law. Drafting legal documents from scratch or giving legal opinions is the unauthorized practice of law. When a client has a legal question, refer them to an attorney.</p>`]
  ],
  terms: [
    ["Land", "The earth's surface, the area below it to the center of the earth, the air above it, and things permanently attached by nature."],
    ["Real estate", "Land plus all permanent man-made improvements attached to it."],
    ["Real property", "Real estate plus the bundle of legal rights that come with ownership."],
    ["Personal property", "Movable items that are not real property; also called personalty or chattels."],
    ["Improvement", "Any man-made addition to land, such as a building, fence, road or utility line."],
    ["Bundle of legal rights", "The rights of ownership: possession, control, enjoyment, exclusion and disposition."],
    ["Air rights", "The right to use the space above the surface of land."],
    ["Subsurface rights", "Rights to what lies below the surface, such as minerals, oil and gas; also called mineral rights."],
    ["Riparian rights", "Rights of owners whose land borders a flowing river or stream."],
    ["Littoral rights", "Rights of owners whose land borders a large, still body of water such as a lake or ocean."],
    ["Prior appropriation", "A water-rights doctrine where the state grants rights to the first party to put water to beneficial use."],
    ["Fixture", "An item of personal property that has been attached to real property so that it becomes part of the real property."],
    ["Trade fixtures", "Items a commercial tenant installs for business use; they remain the tenant's personal property and may be removed."],
    ["Emblements", "Annual crops produced by labor; they are personal property of the tenant who planted them."],
    ["Severance", "Changing real property to personal property by detaching it, such as cutting timber."],
    ["License law", "A state's statute and rules governing who must hold a real estate license and how licensees must act."]
  ],
  scenarios: [
    ["The missing chandelier", "A seller's grandmother gave her a crystal chandelier, which she hung in the dining room. At the final walk-through, the buyer sees a bare bulb. The chandelier was wired into the ceiling, so it was a fixture and should have transferred with the house. The seller should have excluded it in writing in the sales contract, or replaced it before listing. Good practice: ask sellers early which attached items they plan to keep, and put it in the contract."]
  ],
  traps: [
    "When the fixture tests conflict, a written <strong>agreement</strong> between the parties wins. Intention is the next strongest test.",
    "[[Trade fixtures]] stay personal property. If the tenant leaves them after the lease ends, they may become the landlord's property through accession.",
    "[[Riparian rights|Riparian]] = rivers and streams (flowing). [[Littoral rights|Littoral]] = lakes and oceans (still). Remember: “littoral” sounds like “lake shore.”",
    "Area preference (situs) is an <em>economic</em> characteristic, not a physical one.",
    "A refrigerator that plugs in is usually personal property. A built-in refrigerator is usually a fixture."
  ],
  quiz: [
    ["Real estate is best defined as:", ["Land only", "Land plus permanent man-made improvements", "Land plus the bundle of legal rights", "Only buildings"], 1, "[[Real estate]] adds man-made improvements to land. Adding legal rights gives you [[real property]]."],
    ["Which is NOT part of the bundle of legal rights?", ["Possession", "Exclusion", "Disposition", "Taxation"], 3, "Taxation is a power of government, not an owner's right. The bundle includes possession, control, enjoyment, exclusion and disposition."],
    ["A tenant who runs a bakery installs commercial ovens. The ovens are:", ["Fixtures that belong to the landlord", "Trade fixtures that the tenant may remove", "Emblements", "Real property"], 1, "Equipment installed for a business is a [[trade fixtures|trade fixture]] and stays the tenant's personal property."],
    ["When the legal tests for a fixture point in different directions, which controls?", ["Method of attachment", "Cost of the item", "A written agreement between the parties", "Who paid for installation"], 2, "A written agreement between buyer and seller (or landlord and tenant) overrides the other fixture tests."],
    ["An owner whose land borders a river has:", ["Littoral rights", "Riparian rights", "Air rights", "Prior appropriation rights"], 1, "[[Riparian rights]] attach to land along flowing water such as rivers and streams."],
    ["Cutting down trees and selling them as lumber is an example of:", ["Annexation", "Accretion", "Severance", "Avulsion"], 2, "[[Severance]] turns real property (standing trees) into personal property (lumber)."],
    ["Which characteristic explains why a court can order specific performance of a real estate contract?", ["Immobility", "Uniqueness", "Indestructibility", "Scarcity"], 1, "Because every parcel is unique, money damages may not make a buyer whole, so courts can force the sale."],
    ["The most important economic characteristic affecting value is:", ["Area preference", "Indestructibility", "Immobility", "Permanence of investment"], 0, "Area preference, or situs, reflects people's desire for a location and has the greatest effect on value."],
    ["A tenant farmer planted corn, and the lease ended before harvest. The corn:", ["Belongs to the landlord", "Is an emblement the tenant may harvest", "Is a fixture", "Must be split equally"], 1, "[[Emblements]] are annual crops that belong to the tenant who planted them, who may return to harvest."],
    ["Slow buildup of soil along a riverbank that adds to an owner's land is called:", ["Avulsion", "Erosion", "Accretion", "Reliction"], 2, "Accretion is the gradual addition of land. Sudden loss from a flood or storm is avulsion."]
  ]
});

M({
  id: 3,
  title: "Land Use Controls and Property Development",
  summary: "How government planning, zoning, building codes and subdivision rules, along with private deed restrictions, shape what can be built and where. Includes an introduction to real estate investment.",
  objectives: [
    "Explain how police power supports public land-use controls.",
    "Distinguish a nonconforming use, a variance and a conditional-use permit.",
    "Describe the purpose of building codes and certificates of occupancy.",
    "Explain how private controls such as covenants and HOA rules are created and enforced.",
    "Identify basic investment concepts: leverage, cash flow, depreciation and tax-deferred exchanges."
  ],
  sections: [
    ["Why government controls land use", `<p>State and local governments regulate land under their [[police power]]: the right to pass laws that protect public health, safety, morals and general welfare. Land-use controls must be reasonable and applied equally. If a regulation goes so far that it takes away all reasonable use, it may be a “taking” that requires compensation.</p>
<p>Most communities start with a [[master plan]] (comprehensive plan). It is a long-range blueprint for growth that guides zoning, roads, parks, schools and utilities.</p>`],
    ["Zoning", `<p>[[Zoning]] ordinances divide a community into districts and set the permitted uses in each: residential, commercial, industrial, agricultural and mixed use. They also control lot size, building height, density and [[setback|setbacks]] (how far buildings must sit from property lines).</p>
<p>Zoning has built-in flexibility tools that show up on every exam:</p>
<ul>
<li>A [[nonconforming use]] is a use that was legal before a zoning change but no longer fits. It is usually allowed to continue (“grandfathered”), but often cannot be expanded or rebuilt if destroyed.</li>
<li>A [[variance]] allows an owner to depart from the strict terms of zoning, such as a smaller setback, when following the rule would cause unusual hardship. The owner must show the hardship was not self-created.</li>
<li>A [[conditional-use permit]] (special-use permit) allows a use that the zone permits only under conditions, such as a church or school in a residential area.</li>
<li><strong>Spot zoning</strong> rezones a single parcel to benefit one owner in a way that does not fit the master plan. It is generally illegal.</li>
<li>A <strong>buffer zone</strong> such as a park separates very different uses, like homes and factories.</li>
<li><strong>Downzoning</strong> changes a zone to a less intense use, for example commercial to residential.</li>
</ul>
<p>Owners who disagree with a decision appeal to a <strong>zoning board of adjustment</strong> (board of appeals).</p>`],
    ["Building codes and subdivision rules", `<p>[[Building codes]] set minimum construction standards for structure, electrical, plumbing and fire safety. A permit is required before construction, and inspections follow. When the work is finished and approved, the local government issues a [[certificate of occupancy]] stating the building may be lived in or used.</p>
<p>A developer who splits land into lots must follow <strong>subdivision regulations</strong> covering streets, utilities, drainage and lot sizes. The developer records a [[plat]], a detailed map showing each lot, street and easement. <strong>Density</strong> is the number of homes per acre. <strong>Cluster zoning</strong> groups homes closer together to preserve shared open space, and a <strong>planned unit development</strong> (PUD) mixes housing types and uses under a single plan.</p>`],
    ["Private land-use controls", `<p>Owners and developers can limit land use privately with [[deed restrictions]] and <strong>covenants, conditions and restrictions</strong> ([[CC&Rs|CC&amp;Rs]]). A subdivision developer typically records CC&amp;Rs that bind every lot, covering things like minimum home size, fence styles and pets. A [[homeowners association]] (HOA) often enforces them and collects dues.</p>
<p>Private restrictions are enforced by a court order called an <strong>injunction</strong>. A neighbor who waits too long to object may lose the right to enforce under the doctrine of [[laches]]. Restrictions that discriminate based on race, religion or other protected classes are illegal and unenforceable.</p>
<p>When a private restriction and a public law conflict, the <strong>more restrictive</strong> one generally applies.</p>`],
    ["Regulation of land sales", `<p>The federal Interstate Land Sales Full Disclosure Act protects buyers of undeveloped lots sold across state lines. Developers of subdivisions of 100 or more lots generally must register with the federal government and give each buyer a <strong>property report</strong> before signing. A buyer who did not receive the report may be able to cancel the purchase.</p>`],
    ["Real estate investment basics", `<p>Investors buy property for income, appreciation and tax benefits.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Appreciation</strong> is an increase in value over time; inflation alone can drive some of it.</li>
<li>[[Leverage]] means using borrowed money to buy property. It magnifies both gains and losses.</li>
<li><strong>Cash flow</strong> is income left after operating expenses and debt payments.</li>
<li>[[Depreciation]] (cost recovery) lets investors deduct the cost of buildings, but not land, over time for tax purposes. Residential rental property is generally recovered over 27.5 years and commercial over 39 years.</li>
<li>A [[1031 exchange]] lets an investor defer capital gains tax by trading one investment property for another “like-kind” property.</li>
</ul>
<p>Investment property is less <strong>liquid</strong> than stocks: it can take months to sell. A real estate investment trust (REIT) lets people invest in real estate through shares.</p>`]
  ],
  terms: [
    ["Police power", "Government's power to regulate property to protect public health, safety, morals and welfare."],
    ["Master plan", "A community's long-range comprehensive plan for growth and land use."],
    ["Zoning", "Local laws dividing land into districts with permitted uses and building standards."],
    ["Setback", "The required distance between a building and the property line."],
    ["Nonconforming use", "A use that was lawful before a zoning change but no longer complies; usually allowed to continue."],
    ["Variance", "Permission to depart from zoning requirements because strict compliance would cause unusual hardship."],
    ["Conditional-use permit", "Approval for a special use the zone allows only under conditions; also called a special-use permit."],
    ["Building codes", "Minimum standards for construction, plumbing, electrical and fire safety."],
    ["Certificate of occupancy", "A document issued after final inspection stating a building is fit to occupy."],
    ["Plat", "A recorded map of a subdivision showing lots, streets and easements."],
    ["Deed restrictions", "Private limits on land use written into a deed."],
    ["CC&Rs", "Covenants, conditions and restrictions recorded by a developer that bind every lot in a subdivision."],
    ["Homeowners association", "An organization of owners that enforces community rules and maintains common areas."],
    ["Laches", "Loss of a legal right because a person waited too long to enforce it."],
    ["Leverage", "Using borrowed money to finance an investment."],
    ["Depreciation", "For appraisal, a loss in value; for taxes, the deduction of a building's cost over its useful life (cost recovery)."],
    ["1031 exchange", "A tax-deferred trade of one investment property for another like-kind property."]
  ],
  scenarios: [
    ["The corner store in a residential zone", "Mr. Ortiz has run a small grocery since 1985. In 2010 the city rezoned his block residential. His store is now a nonconforming use. He may keep operating, but when he asks to double the store's size, the city denies it, because nonconforming uses usually cannot be expanded. If a fire destroys more than a set percentage of the building, local rules may prevent him from rebuilding it as a store."],
    ["Asking for a variance", "The Lees want to add a garage, but their odd triangular lot makes it impossible to meet the 25-foot side setback. They apply for a variance and show the hardship comes from the lot's shape, not from something they created. The board approves a 15-foot setback."]
  ],
  traps: [
    "A [[variance]] is about hardship from the property itself. A [[conditional-use permit]] is about a use the zoning already contemplates. A [[nonconforming use]] existed before the rule.",
    "When public and private controls conflict, the more restrictive one controls.",
    "Zoning is created under police power, not eminent domain. No compensation is owed for ordinary zoning.",
    "Land is never depreciated for tax purposes. Only buildings and improvements are.",
    "Spot zoning that benefits one owner and conflicts with the master plan is generally illegal."
  ],
  quiz: [
    ["Zoning ordinances are an exercise of which governmental power?", ["Eminent domain", "Police power", "Taxation", "Escheat"], 1, "[[Zoning]] and building codes come from [[police power]], the right to protect public health, safety and welfare."],
    ["A gas station existed before the area was rezoned residential and is allowed to keep operating. It is a:", ["Variance", "Conditional use", "Nonconforming use", "Spot zone"], 2, "A use that was legal before the zoning change and is grandfathered is a [[nonconforming use]]."],
    ["An owner with a uniquely shaped lot asks to build closer to the line than the setback allows. The owner needs a:", ["Variance", "Certificate of occupancy", "Nonconforming use", "Building permit only"], 0, "A [[variance]] relieves hardship caused by the property's unusual characteristics."],
    ["Which document shows that a newly built home has passed final inspection and may be lived in?", ["Plat", "Building permit", "Certificate of occupancy", "Deed restriction"], 2, "A [[certificate of occupancy]] is issued after final inspection approves the building for use."],
    ["A subdivision's rules require all homes to have at least 2,000 square feet. The city's zoning requires 1,500. Which rule applies?", ["The zoning, because public law always wins", "The 2,000-square-foot restriction, because it is more restrictive", "Neither, because they conflict", "Whichever the buyer prefers"], 1, "When public and private controls conflict, the more restrictive one applies."],
    ["A neighbor waited eight years to object to a fence that violated the CC&Rs. A court refuses to enforce the rule. This is an example of:", ["Laches", "Escheat", "Variance", "Downzoning"], 0, "[[Laches]] bars a claim when someone delays unreasonably in enforcing their rights."],
    ["A recorded map showing lots, streets and easements in a subdivision is a:", ["Survey", "Plat", "Master plan", "Deed"], 1, "A [[plat]] is the subdivision map recorded in the public records."],
    ["Using borrowed funds to purchase an investment property is called:", ["Equity", "Leverage", "Liquidity", "Appreciation"], 1, "[[Leverage]] uses other people's money to increase potential return, and also risk."],
    ["For income tax purposes, which part of a rental property can be depreciated?", ["Land only", "The building only", "Both land and building", "Neither"], 1, "Land does not wear out, so only buildings and improvements are depreciable."],
    ["Under federal law, a developer selling 100 or more unimproved lots across state lines generally must give buyers a:", ["Certificate of occupancy", "Property report", "Plat map", "Variance"], 1, "The Interstate Land Sales Full Disclosure Act requires a property report before the buyer signs."]
  ]
});

M({
  id: 4,
  title: "Interests and Estates in Land",
  summary: "The government's four powers over private property, the different estates (ownership interests) a person can hold, and encumbrances such as easements, liens and encroachments.",
  objectives: [
    "Name the four governmental powers over private property.",
    "Distinguish freehold estates from leasehold estates.",
    "Explain fee simple, defeasible fee and life estates, including remainder and reversion.",
    "Identify types of easements and how they are created and ended.",
    "Define liens, encroachments and licenses as encumbrances."
  ],
  sections: [
    ["Government powers: PETE", `<p>Private ownership is always subject to four government powers. Remember them as <strong>PETE</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Police power</strong>: regulate use for public welfare (zoning, building codes).</li>
<li>[[Eminent domain]]: take private property for public use with <em>just compensation</em>. The legal process is called <strong>condemnation</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Taxation</strong>: charge property taxes to fund government.</li>
<li>[[Escheat]]: if an owner dies with no will and no heirs, the property passes to the state.</li>
</ul>`],
    ["Estates in land", `<p>An [[estate]] is the degree, quantity and duration of an ownership interest. There are two broad groups:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Freehold estates</strong> last for an unknown length of time. They are ownership.</li>
<li><strong>Leasehold estates</strong> (non-freehold) last for a set or determinable time. They are a tenant's right to possess, covered in Module 14.</li>
</ul>
<p>The fullest freehold is [[fee simple absolute]]. It is unlimited in duration, can be sold or inherited, and gives the owner the entire bundle of rights. Most homeowners own this way.</p>
<p>A [[fee simple defeasible]] is ownership that can be lost if a condition is broken:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fee simple determinable</strong> uses words like “so long as” or “while.” If the use stops, ownership ends automatically and returns to the grantor (a possibility of reverter).</li>
<li><strong>Fee simple subject to a condition subsequent</strong> uses words like “on the condition that.” If the condition is broken, the grantor must take legal action to reclaim the property (a right of reentry).</li>
</ul>`],
    ["Life estates", `<p>A [[life estate]] lasts only for the lifetime of a named person.</p>
<ul>
<li>In a <strong>conventional life estate</strong>, the owner (the life tenant) holds the property for their own lifetime.</li>
<li>In a life estate <strong>pur autre vie</strong>, the estate is measured by the life of a different person. Example: “to Ana for the life of her mother.”</li>
</ul>
<p>When the measuring life ends, the property goes to someone else:</p>
<ul>
<li>If it returns to the original owner or their heirs, that is a [[reversion]].</li>
<li>If it goes to a named third person, that person holds a [[remainder]] interest.</li>
</ul>
<p>The life tenant can live in, rent or sell the life estate, but anyone who buys it only gets it until the measuring life ends. The life tenant must not commit <strong>waste</strong> (damage that harms the future owner's interest).</p>
<p>Some estates are created by law rather than by a deed. Historically, <strong>dower</strong> and <strong>curtesy</strong> gave a surviving spouse a life interest in property, and many states (including Georgia) still use a <strong>homestead</strong> concept, mainly to provide property-tax exemptions or protection from some creditors for a primary residence.</p>`],
    ["Encumbrances", `<p>An [[encumbrance]] is any claim, lien, charge or liability attached to property that may lower its value or limit its use. Encumbrances fall into two groups:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Liens</strong> are money claims, such as mortgages, tax liens and mechanic's liens (Module 7).</li>
<li><strong>Encumbrances that affect physical condition or use</strong>, such as restrictions, easements, licenses and encroachments.</li>
</ul>`],
    ["Easements", `<p>An [[easement]] is a right to use another person's land for a specific purpose without owning it, such as a driveway or power line.</p>
<ul>
<li>An [[easement appurtenant]] involves two neighboring parcels. The parcel that benefits is the <strong>dominant tenement</strong>; the parcel that carries the burden is the <strong>servient tenement</strong>. It “runs with the land” and transfers automatically when either parcel sells.</li>
<li>An [[easement in gross]] benefits a person or company rather than a neighboring parcel. Utility easements are the most common example. Commercial easements in gross can be transferred; personal ones usually end at death.</li>
<li>An <strong>easement by necessity</strong> is created by law when a parcel has no access to a road (it is landlocked) and was once part of a larger tract.</li>
<li>An [[easement by prescription]] is gained by using someone else's land openly, continuously, without permission and adversely for the time set by state law.</li>
<li>A <strong>party wall</strong> is a shared wall on the line between two properties, with each owner holding an easement in the other's half.</li>
</ul>
<p>Easements end by merger (one person comes to own both parcels), release, abandonment, end of the purpose, or court action.</p>`],
    ["Licenses and encroachments", `<p>A [[license]] is personal permission to enter someone's land for a specific purpose, such as a concert ticket or permission to fish. It can be revoked and ends when either party dies or the land is sold.</p>
<p>An [[encroachment]] occurs when a building, fence or other improvement extends onto a neighbor's property. Encroachments are usually discovered by a <strong>survey</strong> and can make title unmarketable. If left alone long enough, an encroachment may ripen into a prescriptive easement.</p>`]
  ],
  terms: [
    ["Eminent domain", "Government's right to take private property for public use after paying just compensation."],
    ["Escheat", "Transfer of property to the state when an owner dies without a will or heirs."],
    ["Estate", "The degree, quantity, nature and duration of an ownership interest in land."],
    ["Fee simple absolute", "The most complete form of ownership: unlimited in duration and freely transferable."],
    ["Fee simple defeasible", "Ownership that can be lost if a stated condition is broken."],
    ["Life estate", "A freehold estate that lasts only for the lifetime of a named person."],
    ["Reversion", "The future interest of the original owner who gets property back when a life estate ends."],
    ["Remainder", "The future interest of a third party who receives property when a life estate ends."],
    ["Encumbrance", "Any claim, lien, charge or limitation that affects title or use of property."],
    ["Easement", "A right to use another's land for a specific purpose."],
    ["Easement appurtenant", "An easement that benefits a neighboring parcel and runs with the land."],
    ["Easement in gross", "An easement that benefits a person or company rather than a parcel, such as a utility line."],
    ["Easement by prescription", "An easement acquired through open, continuous, adverse use for the period set by state law."],
    ["License", "Revocable personal permission to enter land for a specific purpose."],
    ["Encroachment", "An improvement that illegally extends onto another's property."]
  ],
  scenarios: [
    ["A life estate in the family home", "Martha deeds her house to her son Leo but keeps a life estate for herself. Martha may live there for the rest of her life, and Leo holds the remainder. Martha decides to sell her life estate to an investor. The investor can use the house only until Martha dies; then Leo takes full ownership. Meanwhile, Martha cannot tear down the garage or let the roof rot, because that would be waste that harms Leo's interest."],
    ["The shared driveway", "Two homes share a driveway that sits entirely on Lot A. Lot B's owner has an easement appurtenant. Lot B is the dominant tenement, Lot A is the servient tenement. When Lot A sells, the new owner must still allow Lot B to use the driveway, because the easement runs with the land."]
  ],
  traps: [
    "Eminent domain requires <em>just compensation</em>. Police power (zoning) does not.",
    "The <strong>servient</strong> tenement <em>serves</em> the other parcel; it carries the burden.",
    "A [[license]] is not an interest in land and can be revoked. An [[easement]] is an interest in land.",
    "If property goes back to the original grantor, it is a [[reversion]]. If it goes to someone else, it is a [[remainder]].",
    "Buying a life estate only gives you the property until the measuring life ends, no matter how much you paid."
  ],
  quiz: [
    ["Which governmental power lets a state take ownership of property when the owner dies without a will or heirs?", ["Eminent domain", "Police power", "Escheat", "Taxation"], 2, "[[Escheat]] transfers property of someone who dies with no will and no heirs to the state."],
    ["The highest and most complete form of ownership is:", ["Life estate", "Fee simple absolute", "Fee simple determinable", "Leasehold"], 1, "[[Fee simple absolute]] is unlimited in duration and gives the full bundle of rights."],
    ["A deed reads “to the city so long as it is used as a park.” The city holds a:", ["Fee simple absolute", "Fee simple determinable", "Life estate", "Easement in gross"], 1, "“So long as” language creates a determinable fee that ends automatically if the use stops."],
    ["Jamal deeds a farm “to Ruth for life, then to Sam.” Sam's interest is a:", ["Reversion", "Remainder", "Life estate pur autre vie", "Leasehold"], 1, "A third party who takes after a life estate holds a [[remainder]]."],
    ["In an easement appurtenant, the parcel that benefits from the easement is the:", ["Servient tenement", "Dominant tenement", "Licensee", "Grantor"], 1, "The dominant tenement benefits; the servient tenement is burdened."],
    ["A power company's right to run lines across many properties is an example of:", ["Easement appurtenant", "Easement by necessity", "Easement in gross", "License"], 2, "Utility easements benefit a company, not a neighboring parcel, so they are [[easement in gross|easements in gross]]."],
    ["A neighbor's garage extends three feet onto your lot. This is a(n):", ["Easement", "Encroachment", "License", "Lien"], 1, "An improvement that crosses the property line is an [[encroachment]]."],
    ["Taking private property for a new highway, with payment to the owner, is done through:", ["Escheat", "Police power", "Eminent domain", "Adverse possession"], 2, "[[Eminent domain]] allows public takings for public use with just compensation; the process is condemnation."],
    ["Which is personal, revocable permission to use land?", ["Easement", "License", "Life estate", "Remainder"], 1, "A [[license]] is permission, not an interest in land, and can be revoked."],
    ["An easement ends automatically when the dominant and servient parcels come under one owner. This is called:", ["Merger", "Abandonment", "Condemnation", "Prescription"], 0, "When one person owns both parcels, the easement is no longer needed and ends by merger."]
  ]
});

M({
  id: 5,
  title: "Forms of Ownership",
  summary: "How title can be held by one person, by several people, through trusts and business entities, and through condominiums, cooperatives and time-shares.",
  objectives: [
    "Compare ownership in severalty with co-ownership.",
    "Distinguish tenancy in common from joint tenancy, including the four unities and the right of survivorship.",
    "Explain tenancy by the entirety and community property and where they apply.",
    "Describe how trusts, partnerships, corporations and LLCs hold real estate.",
    "Compare condominium, cooperative and time-share ownership."
  ],
  sections: [
    ["Severalty versus co-ownership", `<p>Title held by one person or one legal entity alone is [[ownership in severalty]]. The word comes from “severed”: the owner is cut off from others. A corporation that owns a building holds it in severalty.</p>
<p>When two or more people own property together, it is <strong>co-ownership</strong> (concurrent ownership). The form chosen matters a great deal when an owner dies or wants to sell.</p>`],
    ["Tenancy in common", `<p>In a [[tenancy in common]], each owner holds an <strong>undivided interest</strong>: each has the right to use the whole property, not a specific room or acre. Shares can be unequal (one owner 70%, another 30%).</p>
<ul>
<li>Each tenant in common can sell, mortgage or will their share without the others' permission.</li>
<li>There is <strong>no right of survivorship</strong>. When a tenant in common dies, their share passes to their heirs or under their will.</li>
<li>In most states, if a deed to two or more people does not say how they hold title, the law presumes a tenancy in common.</li>
</ul>`],
    ["Joint tenancy", `<p>The defining feature of [[joint tenancy]] is the [[right of survivorship]]: when one joint tenant dies, their interest automatically passes to the surviving joint tenants, not to heirs, and without going through probate.</p>
<p>Creating a joint tenancy traditionally requires four unities, remembered as <strong>PTTI</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Possession</strong>: each has an equal, undivided right to possess the whole.</li>
<li><strong>Time</strong>: all owners acquire title at the same time.</li>
<li><strong>Title</strong>: all acquire title by the same deed.</li>
<li><strong>Interest</strong>: all hold equal shares.</li>
</ul>
<p>A joint tenant can sell their share, but the buyer becomes a <strong>tenant in common</strong> with the others, because the time and title unities are broken. The remaining original owners stay joint tenants with each other.</p>
<p>Georgia note: Georgia does not presume survivorship. For co-owners in Georgia to have survivorship rights, the deed should expressly say so, for example “as joint tenants with survivorship and not as tenants in common.”</p>
<p>Co-owners who cannot agree may ask a court for a [[partition]], which divides the property or orders it sold and the money split.</p>`],
    ["Ownership by married couples", `<ul>
<li>[[Tenancy by the entirety]] is a special form for married couples in some states. Each spouse has an equal, undivided interest with right of survivorship, and neither can sell or mortgage without the other. It ends on death, divorce or mutual agreement. Georgia does not recognize this form.</li>
<li>[[Community property]] states (nine states, mostly in the West, such as California, Texas and Arizona) treat most property acquired during marriage as owned equally by both spouses. Property owned before marriage or received by gift or inheritance is <strong>separate property</strong>. Georgia is not a community property state.</li>
</ul>`],
    ["Trusts and business entities", `<p>In a [[trust]], one party (the <strong>trustor</strong>) transfers title to another (the <strong>trustee</strong>) to hold and manage for the benefit of a third (the <strong>beneficiary</strong>). Living trusts and testamentary trusts are common estate-planning tools. A land trust keeps the beneficiary's identity private.</p>
<p>Businesses can also own real estate:</p>
<ul>
<li>A <strong>general partnership</strong>: all partners share management and are personally liable for debts.</li>
<li>A <strong>limited partnership</strong>: one or more general partners run the business, and limited partners invest money but are liable only up to their investment and do not manage.</li>
<li>A <strong>corporation</strong>: a separate legal entity that owns property in severalty. Shareholders are not personally liable, but regular corporations face <em>double taxation</em> (on profits and again on dividends).</li>
<li>A [[limited liability company]] (LLC): combines the liability protection of a corporation with the pass-through taxation of a partnership. It is a popular choice for investors.</li>
<li>A <strong>syndicate</strong> pools investors for a project; a <strong>joint venture</strong> is a partnership for a single project.</li>
</ul>`],
    ["Condominiums, cooperatives and time-shares", `<div class="tbl-wrap"><table>
<tr><th></th><th>Condominium</th><th>Cooperative</th></tr>
<tr><td>What you own</td><td>Fee simple title to your unit, plus an undivided share of the common elements as a tenant in common</td><td>Shares of stock in a corporation that owns the building, plus a proprietary lease for your unit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Is it real or personal property?</td><td>Real property</td><td>Personal property (stock)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Financing</td><td>Your own individual mortgage</td><td>Often one blanket mortgage on the whole building</td></tr>
<tr><td>Risk from neighbors</td><td>Low: each unit is separately owned and taxed</td><td>Higher: if other owners fail to pay their share, everyone is affected</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>A [[condominium]] is created by recording a declaration (master deed), bylaws and a plat. Owners pay monthly assessments to an owners' association that maintains <strong>common elements</strong> such as hallways, roofs and pools. Unpaid assessments can become a lien on the unit.</p>
<p>A [[cooperative]] board often has the power to approve or reject new buyers, within fair housing law.</p>
<p>A [[time-share]] divides ownership or use of a property by time, such as one week a year. A <strong>time-share estate</strong> is a real property interest; a <strong>time-share use</strong> is only a right to use the property for a set number of years.</p>`]
  ],
  terms: [
    ["Ownership in severalty", "Ownership by one person or one legal entity alone."],
    ["Tenancy in common", "Co-ownership with undivided, possibly unequal shares and no right of survivorship."],
    ["Joint tenancy", "Co-ownership with equal shares and a right of survivorship; requires the four unities."],
    ["Right of survivorship", "The automatic passing of a deceased co-owner's interest to the surviving co-owners."],
    ["Partition", "A court action that divides co-owned property or orders it sold and the proceeds split."],
    ["Tenancy by the entirety", "Co-ownership by married spouses with survivorship; neither can convey alone. Not recognized in Georgia."],
    ["Community property", "A system in some states where property acquired during marriage is owned equally by both spouses."],
    ["Trust", "An arrangement where a trustee holds title to property for the benefit of a beneficiary."],
    ["Limited liability company", "A business entity offering liability protection with pass-through taxation."],
    ["Condominium", "Ownership of an individual unit in fee simple plus an undivided interest in common elements."],
    ["Cooperative", "Ownership of shares in a corporation that owns a building, plus a proprietary lease for a unit."],
    ["Time-share", "Ownership or use of a property for a specific period each year."]
  ],
  scenarios: [
    ["Three friends buy a cabin", "Ava, Ben and Cal buy a cabin as joint tenants with survivorship. Ben later sells his one-third share to Dee. Dee becomes a tenant in common holding one-third, while Ava and Cal remain joint tenants with each other on the other two-thirds. When Ava dies, Cal receives her interest by survivorship and now owns two-thirds. Dee still owns one-third as a tenant in common."]
  ],
  traps: [
    "If a deed to two unmarried people is silent on how they hold title, most states presume [[tenancy in common]], not joint tenancy.",
    "A joint tenant can sell their share, but it breaks the joint tenancy only for that share. The buyer becomes a tenant in common.",
    "A corporation owns property in <strong>severalty</strong>, even though it has many shareholders.",
    "Cooperative owners own <em>stock</em> (personal property) and a lease, not real estate. Condominium owners own real property.",
    "Tenants in common can hold unequal shares. Joint tenants must hold equal shares."
  ],
  quiz: [
    ["Ownership by one person or entity alone is called:", ["Tenancy in common", "Severalty", "Joint tenancy", "Community property"], 1, "[[Ownership in severalty]] means ownership by a single person or entity."],
    ["Which form of co-ownership includes the right of survivorship?", ["Tenancy in common", "Severalty", "Joint tenancy", "Cooperative"], 2, "[[Joint tenancy]] carries the [[right of survivorship]]."],
    ["The four unities required for a joint tenancy are:", ["Possession, time, title and interest", "Price, time, title and interest", "Possession, tax, title and income", "Possession, time, trust and interest"], 0, "Remember PTTI: possession, time, title, interest."],
    ["When a tenant in common dies, the share passes to:", ["The surviving co-owners", "The state", "The deceased owner's heirs or devisees", "The lender"], 2, "There is no survivorship in a tenancy in common, so the share passes by will or inheritance."],
    ["Four joint tenants own a lot. One sells her interest to a stranger. The stranger becomes:", ["A joint tenant", "A tenant in common", "The sole owner", "A life tenant"], 1, "The buyer did not take title at the same time or by the same deed, so the buyer is a tenant in common."],
    ["Co-owners who cannot agree on whether to sell can ask a court for:", ["Escheat", "Partition", "Severance", "Probate"], 1, "A [[partition]] action divides the property or forces a sale and divides the proceeds."],
    ["A buyer in a cooperative receives:", ["A deed to the unit", "Shares of stock and a proprietary lease", "A life estate", "A time-share estate"], 1, "[[Cooperative]] owners hold stock in the corporation and a proprietary lease."],
    ["In a condominium, hallways, roofs and the pool are:", ["Owned by the developer forever", "Common elements owned by unit owners as tenants in common", "Owned by the city", "Leased to the owners"], 1, "Each condo owner holds an undivided interest in the common elements."],
    ["Which business form gives investors limited liability and pass-through taxation?", ["General partnership", "Limited liability company", "Sole proprietorship", "C corporation"], 1, "An [[limited liability company|LLC]] combines liability protection with partnership-style taxation."],
    ["In a trust, the party who holds legal title and manages the property is the:", ["Trustor", "Beneficiary", "Trustee", "Grantor"], 2, "The trustee holds title for the benefit of the beneficiary; the trustor creates the trust."]
  ]
});
